{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED. Independent exact-head review of 743f004fe96e2157bfe3bd34df75048d10a54c33 on request/merge-ignore-replacements, under review request e11bf07a and promise 0f2193c5, signing artifact 7b76d83d at cmd/gs. I re-derived every claim at that commit rather than reading the artifact's summary.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD IS. One production change and one test. cmd/gs/main.go:1885-1887 rewrites the argument construction in the shared git helper so every invocation begins --no-replace-objects, ahead of -C. cmd/gs/main_test.go adds TestMergeGuardIgnoresReplacementForApprovedCandidate. Nothing else: git diff --stat main...HEAD is exactly those two files, +40/-1.\n\nTHE PROPERTY IS PROVEN, NOT ASSERTED, AND THIS IS THE PART THAT MATTERS. I reverted the three lines to the previous single-line form in a scratch worktree at this exact head and ran the new test alone. It fails, and it fails with the words that describe the actual attack rather than a missing flag: \"merge landed replacement content \\\"replacement\\\\n\\\" instead of the approved object\". So before this change, a repository-local refs/replace entry pointing the approved candidate at unreviewed content caused gs merge to land that content, under a ratified approval naming the real commit. That is the defect closed, stated as a demonstrated behaviour and not as a plausible one. The test reproduces the attack end to end; it does not check for the flag.\n\nTHE BOUNDARY IS ACTUALLY SHARED. cmd/gs has exactly one non-test exec site, main.go:1888, which is inside this helper. I grepped for exec.Command and exec.CommandContext across cmd/gs: every other hit is in main_test.go. So there is no second path in this package that could resolve an object through a replacement ref, and the fix is at the one place rather than at a call site. Everything cmd/gs does through app.Workspace descends into internal/gitstore, whose own storeGitArguments already prepends the same flag on main from 7754607e. After this merges, both halves of the boundary carry the guard.\n\nHERMETIC. newWorkflowFixture builds its repository under t.TempDir, and the new test's replacement worktree is created as a sibling inside that same temp root, so git replace writes refs/replace only into the fixture. Nothing touches a real repository. The test also pins its own fixture honestly: it asserts that plain git show returns the replacement content and that --no-replace-objects returns the approved content, so a fixture that silently failed to install the replacement would fail loudly rather than pass vacuously.\n\nGATES, run by me at the exact head with a clean worktree. gofmt -l over cmd, internal, host and spike: no output. git diff --check main...HEAD: clean. make vet: clean. make build: both binaries. go test ./... -count=1: 27 packages ok, 0 FAIL, internal/apphost has no test files. go test -race -count=1 ./cmd/gs: ok in 206s. make ui-check is not applicable; the head touches no file under ui/ or internal/service/uidist.\n\nMERGEABILITY, CHECKED AT THE MERGE AND NOT ONLY AT THE HEAD. This head is cut from 0ec3618f and main is now c7054dcd, five merges ahead, so its own tree predates the checkpoint-retry, GitHub-bounds, resident-memory, replacement-boundary and serve-flag merges. Merge-clean is not gate-green, so I did both. git merge-tree main HEAD is clean; I materialised the merge, and git diff --stat main \u003cmerge\u003e is exactly the same two cmd/gs files with the same +40/-1, so nothing landed on main since 0ec3618f is reverted or re-introduced. In particular the merged cmd/gs/main.go differs from main's by precisely the three intended lines and does not resurrect --acknowledge-trusted-processes, which c7054dcd removed. I then ran go test ./cmd/gs -count=1 against the materialised merge: ok in 335s. The artifact claimed a clean merge-tree; I am recording that the merged result is also green, which is the thing main will actually carry.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The affected layer is Surface and composition, cmd/gs, whose row in docs/reference/architecture.md says it reads Git's first-parent merge diff and composes the Workroom receipt, successor artifacts and retirements, and that Git remains outside the Workroom interpreter. This head preserves that contract exactly and changes no other layer. It moves no Workroom concept into a kernel package, adds no dependency, and alters no signature: the helper's inputs, outputs and error text are unchanged, and the error still joins only the caller's arguments so the injected flag does not leak into messages. internal/gitstore and internal/kernel are untouched. No contract changed, so no update to that page is required at this head and none was made.\n\nSECURITY. The affected boundary is the one where a repository-local ref can rename an immutable object, and the change is squarely on the right side of it. Replacement refs are repository-local and unsigned; the sequence names objects by OID, and any command that resolves an OID through a substitution is reading something the log never authorised. With the guard, gs resolves what the sequence names. Scope: the flag disables only replacement-ref substitution and nothing else, so no ordinary Git operation is weakened, no refusal is relaxed, and no configuration, credential, signature or bound is touched. Failure mode is the right one: a repository carrying a hostile replacement now merges the approved object rather than refusing, which is correct, because the approval named the real commit and the replacement was never part of the reviewed world. Placement is right too: applying it in the helper rather than per call site means a future command cannot forget it. I looked for an ordering hazard, since the flag precedes -C: git processes global options in sequence and both orders work, and the test's own testGit call proves the mixed form runs. I found no unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. The production change is three lines for a demonstrated defect and the test is the smallest thing that can prove it; there is no configuration knob, no new helper and no way to disable the guard, which is correct for a property that should never be optional. One observation, deliberately not a request for changes: the make-then-two-appends form could be a single append([]string{\"--no-replace-objects\", \"-C\", repo}, arguments...), which is one line and the shape the file used before. The current form preallocates exactly and matches storeGitArguments in internal/gitstore, so the two boundaries now read alike; that consistency is worth more than the line, and I would not spend a new head on it either way.\n\nMergeable. The head is pushed and its local, tracking and origin refs agree; the working tree is clean; artifact 7b76d83d is live at cmd/gs, ordinarily stale, not world-stale, and re-anchored on the current successor deb7a1d6; and this approval names it so a receipt may retire that predecessor.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7b76d83d39c7216464116e1fb38cbf56630a2759","head":"743f004fe96e2157bfe3bd34df75048d10a54c33","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:551b3d133dd7f5de2745921deb319988f08adb45, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:963745b8a557558de54fe9dd5d249ddf745691df, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a69ea74db59fcb89183001022bc7b61313b01587, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c654df30f3a14cedaa78cf0721fea45a7ab4165e","verdict":"approved"}}